  "improvement": {
    "owning_skills": [
      "stream-processing-design",
      "database-performance",
      "concurrency-correctness",
      "infrastructure-change-safety"
    ],
    "mechanism": "Extend stream-processing-design retained-state analysis to count future additions and retractions, not only visible rows. A replica expiration optimization may discard future work only when the system prevents that work from becoming definite before a scheduled rebuild. Extend database-performance cleanup review to validate NULL semantics, stable delete keys, maintained indexes and total completed goodput including maintenance. The newest log design also shows that final protocol correctness does not establish correctness during mixed old and new client or isolation mode rollouts. Coordinate predecessor locking against truncation, initialization identity, client fencing and staged isolation switches under the actual storage invariant.",
    "reading_and_contract_limits": "The replica expiration article, complete transaction processing article including its correction appendix, and newly discovered linearizable log article including rollout and results were read. Their code and reported benchmarks were not executed. The transaction design is explicitly educational. The newest log article reports a Lean 4 proof and database benchmark, neither independently executed here. Its product rollout flag defaults off and applies after upgrading and fencing old clients, as described for version 26.33 or later; it is not a general instruction to lower isolation. Replica expiration depends on supported object types, wall-clock mapping and scheduled restarts; its article excludes self-managed deployments. The transaction appendix corrects earlier whole-row NULL deletion and performance accounting mistakes.",
    "discriminating_case": "Use a temporal result with a far-future retraction and restart beyond its proposed retention horizon; require proof that omitted updates cannot become visible before rebuild. Separately, clean up transaction rows containing NULL values, and require stable identity deletion plus an independently expected retained-state result. A lower visible row count or stage rate alone is insufficient."
  },
